"== Geometric #01, layout first =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: Three perfect circles intersect to create a clean geometric structure. The circles remain black while only the three overlapping lenses carry rich cobalt blue, making the intersection itself the artwork. Pure, intelligent, crisp. Create exceptional contemporary Geometric tattoo artwork where geometry is the primary visual language and the human body actively determines the artwork. First understand the anatomy of the stated placement including curvature, centerline, width, taper, muscle direction, joint movement, rotation and neighboring anatomical planes. Construct the artwork specifically for that surface. Shoulders should exploit the rounded deltoid. Forearms may use rotation and changing circumference. Elbows and knees may become active centers, interruptions or transformation points. Sternum and chest pieces may use the bodys central axis and bilateral structure. Spine designs may build from or deliberately challenge the spinal centerline. Calf designs should respond to taper. Thigh and hip pieces should exploit broad curved surfaces. Rib designs should travel naturally with the torso. Explore circles, lines, grids, arcs, triangles, polygons, polyhedra, radial structures, tessellations, repetition, contour systems, symmetry, controlled asymmetry, scale progression, rotation, transformation, interference patterns, optical illusion, impossible objects, architectural geometry and mathematical relationships. Sacred geometry and mandala structures may appear selectively but must not define the category. Do not repeatedly create polygon animals, connected diamonds, random triangles or generic geometric flash. Geometry may exist alone or interact meaningfully with animals, insects, flowers, trees, ocean, landscape, architecture, cosmic imagery and other subjects. When another subject appears, geometry should transform, organize, fracture, distort, reveal, contain or interact with it rather than simply sit behind it. COLOR IS ENCOURAGED THROUGHOUT THE COLLECTION. FIRST CREATE AN EXCEPTIONAL GEOMETRIC TATTOO. THEN DECIDE HOW COLOR MAKES THAT PARTICULAR ARTWORK BETTER. Use color to create depth, transparency, transformation, movement, atmosphere, hierarchy, light, separation between systems or a deliberate interruption inside otherwise black geometry. Most colorful tattoos should use controlled palettes of one to four important colors rather than every available color. Explore sophisticated combinations such as cobalt and orange, turquoise and coral, emerald and gold, burgundy and pink, violet and turquoise, red and black, or one electric color against black and natural skin. Broader spectrum color is allowed when the actual artistic concept requires it. Do not turn Geometric into rainbow geometry. Do not create endless jewel-colored diamonds, cubes, polygons or individually colored cells. Do not simply color a black geometric design after creating it. Color must participate in the concept from the beginning. Some exceptional pieces should remain black and natural skin when color would weaken the artwork. Preserve generous negative skin and sufficient spacing so complex structures remain readable. Precision is fundamental. Lines should feel deliberately aligned. Repetition should be coherent. Circles and radial systems must be intentionally designed for curved anatomy rather than accidentally distorted by it. Creativity should come from mathematical relationships, anatomy, movement, transformation, negative space, optical illusion, color, perspective, natural subjects, architecture and impossible spatial relationships. Most pieces should remain wearable small and medium tattoos. Use fewer large pieces and only rare major body compositions. THE STYLE IS GEOMETRIC. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE. THE BODY DETERMINES THE GEOMETRY. THE GEOMETRY REVEALS THE BODY. COLOR BRINGS THE GEOMETRY TO LIFE. In the right panel it is on a woman, inner forearm, where the relatively stable surface preserves the precision of the circles", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("Geometric: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== New School #01, layout first =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A furious blue chameleon lunges forward with one enormous rotating eye dominating the composition. Its tongue shoots toward the viewer while the distant body curls into a compressed spiral of turquoise, purple and acid green. Electric, strange, explosive. Create exceptional contemporary New School tattoo artwork. REALITY IS OPTIONAL. PERSONALITY IS NOT. New School is a visual language built through intentional exaggeration, powerful silhouette, dimensional color, bold variable outlines, extreme perspective, expressive anatomy, graphic clarity and enormous personality. Do not confuse New School with generic cartoons. Do not make everything cute. Do not make everything funny. Do not create childrens illustration. Do not simply enlarge the eyes of an ordinary subject. EXAGGERATE THE THING THAT MAKES THE SUBJECT AWESOME. If the subject is a dragon, exaggerate the head, jaw, horns, claws, wings, movement or scale. If the subject is a skull, exaggerate the jaw, eye sockets, fracture, depth or perspective. If the subject is an animal, exaggerate the feature that expresses its personality. If the subject is a person, exaggerate expression, perspective, fashion, movement, hair, hands or attitude. If the subject is a machine, exaggerate the wheel, engine, lens, grille, exhaust or mechanical form. Do not exaggerate everything equally. CREATE VISUAL HIERARCHY. One dominant element should control the first read. Secondary forms should support it. Use extreme foreshortening when appropriate. Allow foreground hands, claws, mouths, eyes, wheels, horns, lenses and objects to become dramatically larger than distant forms. Compress distant elements. Overlap aggressively. Make the composition feel deep rather than flat. Some artwork should feel as though it projects physically off the body. Use bold confident outlines with intentional variation in line weight. Do not outline every internal detail equally. Outer silhouettes and important forms can carry stronger lines while interior forms use controlled line hierarchy. Use rich dimensional shading. Build convincing volume even when anatomy is impossible. Forms should feel inflated, carved, stretched, compressed, twisted or projected rather than flat. Use strong highlights, reflected color and deep shadows to create dimensionality. COLOR SHOULD FEEL ELECTRIC. Possible palettes include: Crimson and violet. Turquoise and orange. Hot pink and black. Emerald and purple. Cobalt and acid yellow. Burgundy and turquoise. Deep red and toxic green. Magenta and electric blue. Coral and violet. Gold and black. Do not use every available color simply because New School supports bright color. A sophisticated three-color palette can be more powerful than a rainbow. Use black strategically to anchor saturation. Color should create hierarchy, volume, mood and separation. New School can be: Beautiful. Dark. Sexy. Aggressive. Funny. Grotesque. Glamorous. Horrific. Psychedelic. Futuristic. Street influenced. Mythological. Surreal. Mechanical. Emotional. Epic. Do not default to comedy. Dark subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely dark. Beautiful subjects should be allowed to remain genuinely beautiful. Grotesque subjects can be disturbing without becoming childish. The subject can be anything. Animals. People. Women. Men. Faces. Hands. Dragons. Skulls. Skeletons. Demons. Angels. Monsters. Dark fantasy. Horror. Mythology. Cars. Motorcycles. Machines. Fashion. Street culture. Architecture. Nature. Sea life. Birds. Insects. Objects. Food. Space. Surreal worlds. Impossible creatures. Abstract concepts. NEW SCHOOL IS THE STYLE. THE SUBJECT IS NOT THE STYLE. Do not repeat the same smiling animals, giant eyeballs, food characters or cartoon skulls simply because they are common New School imagery. Push artistic ambition. The body must influence the composition. THE BODY CAN BECOME PART OF THE PERSPECTIVE. Use shoulder curvature to increase volume. Use forearm length for approaching or receding forms. Use thighs for extreme depth and large characters. Use calves for vertical movement. Use knees and elbows as rotational centers when appropriate. Use ribs for wrapping forms. Use the chest for aggressive foreground scale. Use the back for rare world-building compositions. Allow creatures and objects to wrap, project, turn, disappear and reappear with the anatomy. Most tattoos should remain realistic projects for actual tattoo clients. Use small and medium pieces throughout the collection. Use large pieces when the idea requires greater depth. Reserve full sleeves, backs and chest projects for exceptional concepts. Avoid visual clutter. New School can contain tremendous information, but the primary silhouette should still read from a distance. The viewer should understand the dominant subject before discovering secondary details. Avoid generic cartoon rendering. Avoid random rainbow palettes. Avoid identical oversized eyes. Avoid endless goofy facial expressions. Avoid childish clip art. Avoid making every animal smile. Avoid making every skull funny. Avoid flat compositions. Avoid equal line weight everywhere. Avoid using graffiti merely as meaningless background. Avoid using color without hierarchy. Avoid confusing New School with Neo-Traditional. NEO-TRADITIONAL generally preserves more believable proportion, decorative illustration and controlled dimensionality. NEW SCHOOL deliberately pushes proportion, perspective, expression, volume and anatomy beyond reality. NEW SCHOOL CAN BREAK REALITY WHILE REMAINING VISUALLY COHERENT. BIG SHAPES. BIG COLOR. BIG DEPTH. BIG PERSONALITY. DARK WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. BEAUTIFUL WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. WEIRD WHEN IT WANTS TO BE. THE STYLE STAYS NEW SCHOOL. THE IMAGINATION HAS NO SPEED LIMIT. In the right panel it is on a woman, outer thigh, giving the oversized eye and projecting tongue room to create strong depth", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("New School: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== Pets #01, layout first =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A senior Golden Retriever looks directly toward the viewer in soft Black and Grey Realism. The grey muzzle, slightly cloudy eyes and uneven fur around the ears are preserved rather than beautified away. The portrait fades naturally into skin below the chest so nothing feels like a photograph pasted onto the body. Loving, warm, honest. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on a specific beloved pet. PETS = WHO I LOVE. The emotional subject is not a generic species. It is one individual animal. The example animal shown in each concept is only an example. NEVER LET THE EXAMPLE PET BECOME THE DESIGN. THE DESIGN MUST STILL WORK WHEN THE CUSTOMER REPLACES THE EXAMPLE WITH THEIR OWN PET. If the concept shows a Doberman, the underlying artistic idea should still work with a Golden Retriever, mixed-breed dog, cat, rabbit, horse, bird or another companion animal whenever logically appropriate. Rotate pets continuously. Do not create a catalog dominated by one or two popular breeds. Include a broad natural mix of dogs, cats, horses, rabbits, birds, reptiles, small mammals and unusual companion animals. Within dogs, rotate breeds, sizes, coat types, ages and appearances. Within cats, rotate coat patterns, breeds, body types and ages. Include mixed-breed animals. Include senior animals. Include young animals. Include rescued animals. Include animals with physical characteristics that make them individually recognizable. Do not beautify away identity. If the real dog has one ear that sits differently, preserve it. If the cat has a torn ear, preserve it. If the senior animal has a grey muzzle, preserve it. If the pet has one eye, preserve it. If the animal has an unusual tooth, scar, coat patch, blaze, feather pattern, scale pattern or posture, preserve it. INDIVIDUALITY IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN BREED PERFECTION. Use the full artistic vocabulary of tattooing. Realism. Black and Grey Realism. Colour Realism. Micro Realism. Fine Line. Sketch. Dotwork. Blackwork. Watercolour. Illustrative. Neo-Traditional. New School. Geometric. Negative Space. Ornamental. Surrealism. And intelligent combinations. Do not rotate styles mechanically. Choose a style because it improves the specific idea. A realistic portrait may be right when exact likeness is the emotional center. Sketch may be right when movement or memory is important. Watercolour may be right when softness or color carries the feeling. Blackwork may be right for a dark animal or powerful silhouette. Fine Line may be right for intimacy and restraint. Dotwork may be right for texture. New School may be right for a pet with enormous personality. Ornamental treatment may be right when the pet is being elevated into something precious. The style serves the relationship. Pet tattoos do not always need the pets face. Explore: Actual paw prints. Actual nose prints. Actual collars. Actual tags. Actual toys. Actual blankets. Actual feathers. Actual horseshoes. Favorite objects. Favorite sleeping positions. Favorite places. Favorite walks. Daily routines. Car rides. Windows. Beds. Chairs. Trails. Hands touching fur. Forehead-to-forehead contact. The way an animal looked back. The way it waited. The way it slept. The way it ran. The way it sat beside someone. The ordinary behavior may contain more emotional truth than a formal portrait. DO NOT USE GENERIC PAW PRINTS WHEN THE CONCEPT CALLS FOR THE CUSTOMERS ACTUAL PAW PRINT. DO NOT USE GENERIC TAGS WHEN THE CUSTOMERS REAL TAG CAN BE USED. DO NOT INVENT PERSONAL DETAILS. The design should invite personalization. Multiple pets must form one composition. Do not default to stacking floating animal heads. Show relationships. Pets can sleep together. Run together. Walk together. Touch noses. Share furniture. Occupy the same environment. Interact naturally. If portrait composition is used, connect the portraits through deliberate structure rather than merely arranging heads vertically. MEMORIAL IS PART OF PETS. MEMORIAL IS NOT ALL OF PETS. Living pets should be celebrated just as strongly. Joy does not require comedy. Show companionship. Energy. Trust. Age. Habit. Comfort. Adventure. Home. Personality. Affection. Memorial concepts should avoid automatic cliches. Do not automatically add: Angel wings. Halos. Heavenly clouds. Generic infinity symbols. Generic heartbeat lines. Generic paw icons. RIP text. Clocks. Dates surrounded by decorative filler. These elements can be used when specifically meaningful to the customer, but death itself does not require a visual symbol. Sometimes the empty collar is enough. Sometimes the sleeping position is enough. Sometimes the old tennis ball is enough. Sometimes the grey muzzle is enough. Sometimes the familiar window is enough. Sometimes the animal simply walking away is enough. Design for the body. Do not place a rectangular photograph on skin. Allow portraits to fade naturally. Allow full bodies to follow anatomy. Allow horses to use long placements. Allow birds to open across shoulders. Allow snakes and long-bodied pets to wrap. Allow sleeping animals to form natural curved compositions. Allow multiple pets enough space to interact. Use small tattoos where intimacy benefits from small scale. Use medium tattoos for most portrait and relationship concepts. Use large scale only when the artistic idea deserves it. Do not make everything a portrait. Do not make everything memorial. Do not make everything sentimental. Do not make everything cute. Do not make everything realistic. Do not make everything Fine Line. Do not make everything a dog. Do not make everything a cat. Do not make everything a Golden Retriever. Do not make everything a Maine Coon. Do not make every animal young and perfect. Do not use generic breed stereotypes as personality. The customers animal provides the personality. THE PET IS NOT A SYMBOL UNTIL THE CUSTOMER MAKES IT ONE. THE PET IS FIRST A RELATIONSHIP. The goal of every inspiration image is for someone to think: THAT IS NOT MY PET. BUT I WANT THAT IDEA WITH MY PET. In the right panel it is on a woman, outer upper arm", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u; $out += ("Pets: " + $u) } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
"== Animals #01, layout first =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: A snow leopard moves diagonally down a rocky slope rather than posing for a portrait. Black and Grey Realism resolves the head and shoulders while the spotted body progressively disappears into broken snow and untouched skin. Silent, elusive, powerful. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on animals. ANIMALS = WHAT I LOVE. This category is about fascination with animals themselves. Their movement. Anatomy. Behavior. Texture. Environment. Power. Beauty. Strangeness. Violence. Scale. Survival. DO NOT GENERATE AN ANIMAL PORTRAIT WHEN AN ANIMAL EXPERIENCE WOULD BE MORE INTERESTING. Do not default to a centered animal head looking directly at the viewer. Show animals behaving like animals. Running. Hunting. Swimming. Diving. Flying. Landing. Climbing. Stalking. Feeding. Fighting. Resting. Migrating. Shedding. Emerging. Disappearing. Turning. Striking. Escaping. Moving through environments. DO NOT MAKE THE ANIMAL BEAUTIFUL BY DEFAULT. MAKE IT ALIVE. Beauty is welcome. But beauty is not the requirement. Animals can be: Elegant. Violent. Quiet. Predatory. Ugly. Strange. Ancient. Fragile. Massive. Graceful. Armored. Wet. Dirty. Wounded. Hidden. Colorful. Dark. Terrifying. Peaceful. Do not romanticize every animal. Do not turn every predator into a noble portrait. Do not turn every bird into wings spread symmetrically. Do not turn every snake into a decorative coil. Do not turn every ocean animal into a peaceful blue scene. Do not turn every wildlife tattoo into flowers surrounding an animal head. ROTATE SPECIES AGGRESSIVELY. Include familiar animals. Lion. Lioness. Tiger. Leopard. Jaguar. Panther. Wolf. Fox. Coyote. Bear. Polar bear. Elephant. Gorilla. Orangutan. Rhinoceros. Giraffe. Zebra. Stag. Moose. Bison. Ram. Mountain goat. Wild horse. Wild boar. But do not allow familiar animals to consume the category. Explore birds. Raven. Crow. Eagle. Falcon. Owl. Heron. Crane. Pelican. Flamingo. Hummingbird. Vulture. Peacock. Bat. Explore ocean life. Great white shark. Hammerhead shark. Whale. Orca. Dolphin. Manta ray. Octopus. Squid. Jellyfish. Sea turtle. Seahorse. Deep-sea animals. Explore reptiles and amphibians. Crocodile. Alligator. Cobra. Python. Rattlesnake. Chameleon. Iguana. Komodo dragon. Tortoise. Frog. Salamander. Explore strange and overlooked animals. Pangolin. Axolotl. Praying mantis. Beetles. Cicadas. Spiders. Scorpions. Centipedes. Rare regional wildlife. Explore prehistoric animals when appropriate. Treat them as animals rather than fantasy monsters. Use current scientific understanding when anatomical credibility matters. THE STYLE IS NOT FIXED. Use the full world of tattooing. Realism. Black and Grey Realism. Colour Realism. Fine Line. Sketch. Dotwork. Blackwork. Watercolour. Illustrative. Geometric. Japanese. American Traditional. Neo-Traditional. New School. Negative Space. Surrealism. And intelligent hybrids. Do not rotate styles mechanically. Ask what the animal gives the artist. Fur can become brushwork. Feathers can become Blackwork. Scales can become geometry. Spots can become Dotwork. Stripes can become abstraction. Tentacles can become body flow. Wings can become large negative-space compositions. Shells and armor can become texture studies. Translucent animals can become Colour Realism and Watercolour. White animals can be created through untouched skin. Black animals can emerge from darkness. THE ANIMALS ANATOMY SHOULD HELP DESIGN THE TATTOO. A snake can wrap. An octopus can travel around anatomy. A manta ray can own a back. A bird can use the shoulder blades. A giraffe can use vertical anatomy. A crocodile can travel down a leg. A whale can establish enormous scale. A stag can expand through antlers. A jellyfish can descend through long placements. A big cat can move across the thigh. A raven can use shoulder and chest. Do not put every animal inside a rectangular scene. Allow environment and animal to interlock. Use: Snow. Water. Fog. Dust. Rain. Grass. Forest. Rock. Desert. Ocean. Swamp. Night. Fire. Wind. Light. Shadow. Environment should reveal something about the animal. It should not simply become decorative background. REGIONAL WILDLIFE MATTERS. People may love animals because those animals belong to where they are from. Do not assume every animal requires universal symbolism. A coyote can matter because someone grew up hearing coyotes. A pelican can matter because someone lives on the coast. An alligator can matter because it belongs to someones landscape. A whale can matter because someone sees them migrate. The animal does not need a dictionary meaning to deserve a tattoo. DO NOT AUTOMATICALLY ASSIGN CANNED SYMBOLISM. Lion does not automatically mean courage. Wolf does not automatically mean loyalty. Owl does not automatically mean wisdom. Snake does not automatically mean rebirth. If symbolism is important, let the customer define it or explain genuine cultural associations accurately. Nature can become dark without becoming Horror. Explore: Predation. Death. Decay. Shedding. Skeletons. Migration. Survival. Carcasses. Scavengers. Deep water. Darkness. Injury. Transformation. Weather. Extinction. Life cycles. Keep the animal world central. Do not add skulls and demons simply to make an animal tattoo darker. NATURE ALREADY CONTAINS DARKNESS. Surrealism is welcome when the animal remains the hero. A whale can contain a storm. A stags antlers can become forest. A leopards spots can become stars. A zebras stripes can leave its body. A birds wings can contain night. A rhinoceros can become landscape. A manta ray can pass over an impossible city. A snake can become smoke. But do not bury the animal beneath an unrelated concept. The viewer should still think: WHAT AN INCREDIBLE ANIMAL TATTOO. Design for actual human anatomy. Use the body. Wrap when the animal gives you wrapping anatomy. Stretch when the animal gives you length. Expand when the animal gives you wings. Use joints when movement benefits from them. Use broad surfaces for scale. Use small placements for delicate animals. Use large placements only when the concept earns them. Do not make everything enormous. Do not make everything tiny. Do not make everything realistic. Do not make everything Blackwork. Do not make everything colorful. Do not make everything dark. Do not make everything pretty. Do not make everything safe. THE CATEGORY TELLS US THE DOORWAY. IT DOES NOT TELL US HOW FAR WE ARE ALLOWED TO GO ONCE WE ARE INSIDE. THE ANIMAL IS THE HERO. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE. In the right panel it is on a woman, outer thigh", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
